<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikael Sehul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1691–<a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikael Sehul was a Tigrayan lord who dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> in the late 18th century. Having built a strong regional state in Tigray that controlled the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, had Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a> killed in 1769 and installed and removed emperors at will. His seizure of power is usually taken as the beginning of the Era of Princes. Driven out of Gondar in 1771, he spent his last years in Tigray.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul.jpg" alt="Mikael Sehul" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Bruce · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ras_Mikael_Sehul_burst_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Blatta Mikael in the district of Na'eder, he was the son of Abeto Hezeqeyas Wolde Hawaryot and Woizero Ishate Mariam, daughter of Azzaz Yakub; both parents claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. He first appears in the record in 1745, when a delegation sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> for a new Abuna was held up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Abuna Yohannes, was held for ransom at Arqiqo. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a> sent a punitive campaign against him, but Mikael was too powerful and was soon forgiven.</p><p>Offended by his superior, Ras Anda Haymanot of Midre Bahri, he fortified Adwa and rebelled, and in 1759 he captured and executed him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> lay on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, and its fees and duties paid for an army of 8000 men armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>. Marriage tied him to the imperial house: Empress Mentewab gave him her daughter Aster, and his son Wolde Hawaryat married another daughter, Altash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a> a quarrel between Mentewab and the emperor's mother, Welete Bersabe, filled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> with rival armies from Qwara and Yejju. Mentewab called on her son-in-law, who arrived with 26,000 men, offered to mediate and took control of the capital. On 22 January 1768 he was made Ras Bitwodad and Enderase of the empire. When Iyoas secretly turned to Fasil and ordered him back to Tigray, Mikael defeated Fasil's army and laid before an assembly of nobles his proof that the emperor had plotted his death. Because the law forbade killing a monarch, the assembly only confined Iyoas, and Mikael then had him strangled with a length of red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p><p>He made Yohannes II emperor, soon got rid of him, and replaced him with Tekle Haymanot II. A rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> met a reign of terror in 1770, but Fasil, Goshu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> and Wand Bewossen of Begemder allied against him, and after the three battles of Sarbakusa he surrendered to Wand Bewossen on 4 June 1771.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a year in prison he returned to Tigray as its governor, either sent by Wand Bewossen or of his own choice. The explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce">James Bruce</a>, who met him, described a lean, lame old man who spoke little. In church affairs he championed the Karva Haymanot doctrine. His son Wolde Samuel, his grandson Wolde Gabriel and his nephew Gabre Maskal followed him in Tigray, each briefly. Some historians date the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>, the age of regional lords and a weakened monarchy, from the murder of Iyoas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikael Sehul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the mid-18th century, as the Gondar emperors lost power, Ras <b>Mikael Sehul</b> built up a strong regional state in Tigray, the old heartland of Aksum, controlling the trade route to Massawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a></li></ul></section>
